[University of Massachusetts at Amherst]

           University Computing Services
               Computer Use Policy
                 March 19, 1993
 
1.0 Preface
 
	This document describes the guidelines that UCS uses in 
providing services to its Clients.  The UCS Director has 
great leeway within these guidelines to assure that services 
operate smoothly.
 
	Many people and organizations, local and distant, use 
UCS Services.  Within this document we refer to this 
collective group as Clients.  This document is intended to 
tell Clients what they can expect from UCS and what UCS 
expects from them.
	
2.0 UCS responsibilities
 
	UCS operates a variety of services for its clients, and 
assures its clients that these services will be reliable.  
By reliable we mean that the services will operate according 
to schedule, efficiently, correctly, and securely.  UCS will 
take any action necessary, including  unannounced inspection 
of a client's files, jobs, and terminal sessions, denial of 
access privileges, and instigation of formal University 
disciplinary procedures, in order to protect the reliability 
of the services and the security of information.
 
	In addition to the policies and responsibilities 
discussed in this document, UCS will also rely on other 
relevant University documents, such as "Undergraduate Rights 
and Responsibilities," when appropriate.
 
2.1 Distributed access 
 
	UCS maintains connections to various networks, and 
considers these connections to be among the most vital of 
our services.  UCS has a responsibility to help maintain the 
reliability of computers and networks at other sites.  UCS 
will treat any attempt to compromise the reliability of 
another computing system through UCS network connections as 
if the attempt were directed at UCS computers.
 
2.2 Moderate security
 
	UCS makes every effort to ensure that the information 
in its systems is secure.  By secure we mean that the 
information stored in the computer will be safe from 
unauthorized access, and that UCS won't lose the 
information.  It is important to realize that UCS cannot 
make these guarantees absolute.  Clients have a variety of 
levels of security available, and must choose the level 
appropriate for their own information. 
 
2.3 Privacy
 
	UCS will only inspect the contents of non-public files 
to protect the reliability of its services.  UCS makes no 
attempt to censor any information held on its systems.
 
2.4 Backup
 
	UCS runs backup procedures on its computers every 
evening.  Theoretically, in the event of a computer failure, 
it is possible to recover all information stored in a 
computer at the time of the last backup.  There are, 
however, occasional problems with these restorations which 
can result in other lost information.  
 
 
 
3.0 Client responsibilities
 
	UCS Clients must bear certain responsibilities in order 
to continue using UCS services.  Responsible clients are 
necessary if UCS is to provide reliable services.  UCS 
computer systems are not immune to tampering.  UCS relies on 
its clients to refrain from deliberate attempts to abuse the 
systems.  UCS clients are responsible for contacting UCS 
prior to undertaking any activity which UCS could interpret 
as compromising the reliability and security of computer 
systems and networks.  Several other specific 
responsibilities are described below.  
 
3.1 Proper Use
 
	UCS computers and networks are funded by a variety of 
State, University, student, and commercial sources.  
University employees and students may use UCS services only 
for work done for the University, and only when it is 
appropriate that the work be supported by public funds.  
Personal, for-profit activities are explicitly forbidden 
(except from a commercial account).
 
3.2 Sharable Resource
 
	UCS resources are limited, and shared by many clients.  
To avoid performance problems and/or unnecessary expenses, 
clients must refrain from initiating activities that consume 
an unreasonable amount of resources.
  
3.3 Unauthorized Access
 
	Clients must only access information that belongs to 
them, is permitted to them, or is public.  Clients must not 
attempt to decode, crack, or discover passwords that belong 
to others.  UCS may remove clients who are found to possess 
programs that could be used to access private information 
that belongs to others.   
 
	Clients can assure the privacy of their own information 
by being careful NEVER to share a password with anyone.  Do 
not send passwords through e-mail, because others can easily 
scan e-mail messages looking for them.   
 
3.4 Harassment
 
	UCS clients must not use UCS services to harass others.  
This is considered a serious offense which UCS will pursue 
according to University regulations.  
 
	If a client wishes to make "objectionable" material 
available through UCS services (such as bulletin boards), 
the client must clearly label such material.  UCS considers 
sending unrequested objectionable (as defined by the 
recipient) material to others to be harassment. 
 
	Clients may not use UCS services to support 
inappropriate activities on any network, even if the 
activities don't interfere directly with UCS services.  UCS 
will pursue allegations of inappropriate network activities 
with the utmost diligence.
 
3.5 Back-up files
 
	Clients are responsible for maintaining their own 
multiple, current back-up copies of valuable or critical 
information to insure against inadvertent loss by UCS.
 
3.6 Pay attention to UCS Information
 
	UCS frequently uses its systems to provide its clients 
with important information.  We ask that clients pay 
attention to UCS announcements in order to protect 
themselves from unnecessary difficulties.
 
 

